messages:
Jess Support: Heads-up — customer (acct: acme-inc) reporting repeated tool-calling failures after recent model update. They say our OpenAI-compatible tool call schema used to work and now returns a 400 validation error. Ticket #SUP-4092. Copy of the root error they see: `invalid_request_error: tool_call_validation_failed: expected enum 'priority' to be one of [LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH] but got '{"priority": "low"}'`

Mike Eng: Thanks — can you paste the exact request payload they're sending and the model output that triggered the tool call? Also which SDK/language and SDK version?

Jess Support: Sending example. They call our endpoint via our hosted API with the following body (curl):

```
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/chat/completions \n -H "Authorization: Bearer xxxxx" \n -H "Content-Type: application/json" \n -d '{
   "model": "gpt-4o-mini",
   "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"check order"}],
   "functions": [{
     "name": "lookupOrder",
     "parameters": {
       "type": "object",
       "properties": {
         "order_id": {"type":"string"},
         "priority": {"type":"string","enum":["LOW","MEDIUM","HIGH"]}
       },
       "required": ["order_id"]
     }
   }]
 }'
```

And model returned (trimmed):

```
{
  "tool_call": {
    "name": "lookupOrder",
    "arguments": "{\"order_id\": 12345, \"priority\": \"low\"}"
  }
}
```

Response we bubble up from the runtime: `400 Bad Request - tool schema validation failed: type mismatch for order_id (expected string got integer); enum mismatch for priority (expected uppercase enum)`

Tara Oncall: OK reproduced locally using their payload. Two problems seen: 1) model emitted arguments as a JSON string rather than parsed object (arguments is a string) — older models sometimes returned a parsed object. 2) priority uses lowercase 'low' but schema expects uppercase enum. Both are valid real-world variations and we should tolerate them or coerce.

Mike Eng: This is an OpenAI-compatible tool calling mismatch: some models/agents return `arguments` as a string, or use different casing for enums. Our runtime currently strictly validates against the declared JSON Schema and rejects non-exact types/case. We need an escalation plan.

Jess Support: Customer is blocked — their integration is in prod. They can temporarily wrap with a proxy to coerce types but need a proper fix. They asked if we support backward-compatible enum mapping or auto-parsing of stringified args.

Mike Eng: Proposed quick mitigation (days): accept string-valued `arguments` and attempt to JSON.parse; coerce numeric order_id to string when schema says string; normalize enum strings to uppercase before validation. Longer term (weeks): add a feature flag on model-call to enable lenient tool-call parsing and a per-customer compatibility shim. Also add telemetry to track how often models return stringified arguments or enum casing mismatches.

Tara Oncall: I can push a hotfix to the runtime to JSON.parse arguments if it's a string and attempt gentle coercions. Will also add a warning log and a metric `tool_call.lenient_parse=true` so we can audit customer impact. Patch plan:

1) On receive tool_call: if typeof(arguments) === 'string' -> try JSON.parse, if fails -> reject with clear error and include raw arguments in logs (redact PII).
2) After parsing, run schema validator with a preprocessor that coerces number->string for properties declared as string if the value is numeric, and normalizes enum strings to declared casing (map lowercase->uppercase) only when the enum value matches ignoring case.
3) Expose toggles per-tenant behind feature flag `tools.lenient_validation`.

Code snippet Tara pasted in runtime logs for the quick parse step:

```
// pseudo
function normalizeToolArgs(schema, args) {
  if (typeof args === 'string') {
    try { args = JSON.parse(args); }
    catch(e) { logger.warn('tool args parse failed', { error: e.message }); return null; }
  }
  for (const [k,v] of Object.entries(args)) {
    const prop = schema.properties[k];
    if (!prop) continue;
    if (prop.type === 'string' && typeof v === 'number') args[k] = String(v);
    if (prop.enum && typeof v === 'string') {
      const match = prop.enum.find(e => e.toLowerCase() === v.toLowerCase());
      if (match) args[k] = match; // normalize case
    }
  }
  return args;
}
```

Mike Eng: That looks reasonable for a hotfix. A few extra asks before we ship:
- Add unit tests for nested objects where inner fields may be stringified (e.g., arguments: "{\"filter\": {\"id\": 42}}")
- Ensure we don't silently accept totally different types (eg. an object where string was expected) without a warning
- Add a small performance benchmark — we don't want JSON.parse on every call to add measurable latency for high-throughput customers.

Jess Support: Customer asked about OpenAI-compat docs: they thought this was stable contract. Can we add guidance in docs about models that may return stringified arguments and recommended schema patterns (e.g., prefer order_id as string in all clients)?

Tara Oncall: Yes we'll add doc note and a migration guide. For now I've enabled `tools.lenient_validation` for acme-inc on staging and ran their test suite — all green. I'll schedule the hotfix roll for the next maintenance window and keep you posted.

ops-bot: Incident created: INC-2026-149, severity P2 — hotfix scheduled for 2024-06-13 02:00UTC.

Mike Eng: Postmortem items: 1) Add compatibility shim and feature flag; 2) Add telemetry metrics and dashboards; 3) Update docs and SDK examples to show canonical casing for enums and note that some model outputs may vary; 4) Consider adding an SDK helper that normalizes tool call arguments client-side.

Jess Support: Noted. I'll reply to customer with temporary proxy suggestion and timeline (hotfix window). Keeping ticket SUP-4092 open and will attach reproduction logs. :thumbsup:
